            <ArticleTitle>Long-wavelength infrared solitons in air.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>3614-3617</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1364/OL.42.003614</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Dispersion and optical nonlinearity of atmospheric air in the long-wavelength infrared (LWIR) range are shown to enable unique soliton dynamics in freely propagating laser beams. Analysis of spatiotemporal LWIR waveform evolution in air reveals soliton self-compression scenarios whereby ultrashort LWIR subterawatt pulses can be compressed to single-cycle terawatt field waveforms.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
